3. Core Components
A. Business Owner/Admin Portal
This is the control center.
The owner should see a dashboard such as:
Metric Example
Total Active Loans ₦85,000,000
Today's Expected Collection ₦3,500,000
Today's Collection ₦2,950,000
Outstanding Collection ₦550,000
Bank Credits Today ₦2,800,000
Unreconciled Amount ₦150,000
Overdue Loans ₦4,200,000
Active Customers 3,200
Account Managers 16
The dashboard should support filtering by:
Date → Account Manager → Customer → Loan → Branch/Location → Transaction
Type
4. Account Manager Application
Each Account Manager should have a simple mobile-friendly application.
They should be able to:
Customer
View assigned customers.
Register new customers.
View customer loan history.
View outstanding balance.
Collection
Record payment received.
Select payment method.
Upload evidence where necessary.
Generate/issue receipt.
View today's collection target.
View actual collection.
See customers yet to pay.
Repayment
If the business also gives customers money or processes repayments to customers, the
Account Manager should be able to initiate the appropriate transaction subject to approval.
Daily Summary
At the end of each day:
Expected Collection: ₦1,500,000
Actual Collection: ₦1,350,000
Outstanding: ₦150,000
This immediately gives management visibility.
5. Customer Management
Every customer should have a unique Customer ID.
For example:
CUS-00001245
The customer's profile should contain:
Name
Phone number
Address
Account Manager
Loan history
Current loan
Total amount borrowed
Total amount repaid
Outstanding principal
Interest/charges
Next payment date
Payment history
Transaction history
Customer status
This creates a single source of truth for each customer.
6. Loan Management
Each loan should have its own unique Loan ID.
Example:
LN-2026-0000125
The system should capture:
Principal amount
Interest rate
Interest amount
Processing fee
Disbursement date
Tenure
Repayment frequency
Expected repayment
Number of installments
Next repayment date
Outstanding principal
Outstanding interest
Loan status
Possible statuses:
Pending → Approved → Disbursed → Active → Completed
or
Active → Overdue → Defaulted
7. The Most Important Component: Central Transaction
Ledger
This is where I would place the greatest emphasis.
Do not allow users to simply edit balances.
Instead, every financial event creates a transaction.
For example:
Loan Disbursement
↓
+ ₦500,000
Customer Repayment
↓
- ₦50,000
Customer Repayment
↓
- ₦50,000
Bank Credit
↓
+ ₦100,000
Adjustment
↓
± Amount
The system calculates the customer's balance from these transactions.
This prevents the common problem where:
Customer screen says ₦250,000 outstanding
Account Manager says ₦200,000
Accountant says ₦230,000
Bank statement says something else.
There should be one authoritative ledger.
8. Bank Integration
This should be implemented in phases.
Phase 1 – Manual Bank Statement Upload
For the fastest MVP, allow the administrator to upload:
CSV / Excel / Bank Statement
The system reads:
Transaction date
Amount
Debit/Credit
Narration
Account number
Reference
The system then attempts to match the transaction to a customer.
Phase 2 – Bank API/Webhook
For a more robust solution, integrate with the bank's transaction API or webhook.
BANK
│
│ Credit Alert
▼
Bank API/Webhook
│
▼
Integration Service
│
▼
Transaction Engine
│
▼
Customer/Ledger Matching
│
▼
Real-Time Dashboard
For example, when ₦100,000 enters the company's account:
Bank Credit: ₦100,000
Reference: CUS12345
Customer: John Doe
Loan: LN-2026-00452
Payment: ₦100,000
Outstanding: ₦250,000 → ₦150,000
The dashboard updates automatically.
Important: Do not automatically allocate unidentified bank credits without a controlled
matching/reconciliation process. Unknown transactions should enter an "Unreconciled
Transactions" queue.
9. Reconciliation Engine
This is another critical component.
The system should compare:
Account Manager Collection
against
Bank Credit
against
Customer Payment
against
Ledger Entry
Example:
Description Amount
AM reported collection ₦500,000
Customer receipts ₦500,000
Bank credit ₦450,000
Difference ₦50,000
The system should immediately flag:
🔴 ₦50,000 reconciliation exception
Management can then investigate.
10. Role-Based Access Control
I strongly recommend the following roles:
Super Admin / Business Owner
Full access.
Operations Manager
Customers, loans, collections and reports.
Account Manager
Only assigned customers and transactions.
Finance/Accountant
Bank transactions, reconciliation and financial reports.
Auditor
Read-only access and audit reports.
System Administrator
Technical configuration without unnecessary access to financial operations.
This is particularly important because Account Managers should never be able to
modify historical financial transactions or balances.
11. Audit Trail
Every sensitive action should be logged.
For example:
User: AM-005
Action: Recorded repayment
Customer: CUS-00125
Amount: ₦75,000
Date: 25 Aug 2026, 10:32 AM
Device/IP: Recorded
Transaction ID: TXN-982736
If someone changes or reverses a transaction, the system should record:
Who → What → When → Previous Value → New Value → Reason
Financial records should preferably be reversed rather than deleted.
