13. Recommended Technology Stack
If your objective is speed + scalability + relatively low development complexity, I would
recommend:
Frontend
React / Next.js
For Account Managers, make it a responsive Progressive Web App (PWA) initially
rather than immediately developing separate Android and iOS applications.
This saves significant development time.
Backend
Two good options:
Option A – Node.js + NestJS
or
Option B – .NET 8 / ASP.NET Core
For an enterprise-grade financial application, I particularly like:
React/Next.js + .NET + PostgreSQL
Database
PostgreSQL
It is reliable, transactional and very suitable for financial applications.
Cloud
For speed, you could deploy on:
Microsoft Azure
AWS
Google Cloud
Given your Microsoft background, Azure would be a natural choice.
A simple Azure architecture could use:
Azure Front Door
│
▼
Next.js Web App
│
▼
ASP.NET Core API
│
├── Azure PostgreSQL
├── Azure Service Bus
├── Azure Blob Storage
├── Application Insights
└── Azure Key Vault
The Golden Rule for This Application
I would make this the fundamental design principle:
Never store the customer's balance as the primary source of truth; store the
financial transactions and derive the balance from the ledger.
For example:
Outstanding Balance = Loan Principal + Applicable Charges − Confirmed
Repayments
Every transaction must have:
Transaction ID + Customer ID + Loan ID + Account Manager + Amount + Transaction
Type + Date/Time + Status + Reference
This will make the system significantly easier to audit, reconcile and scale.
What the Business Owner Should Ultimately See
The end product should allow the owner to open the dashboard and immediately answer:
How much did we lend?
How much should we collect today?
How much have we actually collected?
How much has entered the bank?
How much is outstanding?
Which Account Manager is performing?
Which customers are overdue?
Are there unexplained differences between collections and bank credits?
What is our total exposure right now?
That is the real objective of the application—not simply recording transactions, but giving
management a reliable real-time financial position.
My suggested starting point
For the shortest time to market, let's would build:
Next.js/PWA + .NET API + PostgreSQL + Azure, with a central double-entry-inspired
transaction ledger, role-based access, audit trail and manual bank statement import
initially. Then introduce direct bank API/webhook integration after the core transaction
engine has been proven.
That approach gives you a relatively simple first version without compromising the
architecture needed to eventually handle thousands or tens of thousands of customers
and multiple Account Managers.